# Chuggernaut production config — copy to deploy/prod/chuggernaut.env on the Mini
# and fill in. That copy is gitignored. Sourced (set -a) by boot.sh and the
# run-*.sh wrappers, so `${VAR}` references to earlier vars DO expand.
#
# Repo-relative paths (CHANNEL_BINARY, UI_DIST) are derived by the wrappers from
# the checkout location — do NOT set them here.

# --- State lives OUTSIDE the git checkout so CI checkout / git pull never touch it.
# Set CHUG_DATA to the DEPLOY USER's home (the account the launchd services run as).
CHUG_DATA=/Users/<you>/chuggernaut-data
KEYS_DIR=${CHUG_DATA}/keys
REPOS_ROOT=${CHUG_DATA}/repos
BACKUP_DEST=${CHUG_DATA}/backups
# Served-UI host dir: the native api serves the SPA from here (UI_DIST, set by
# run-api.sh) so web-publish jobs can swap the static front end without a
# redeploy (README §7). update.sh builds web/ and seeds it on every deploy.
UI_ROOT=${CHUG_DATA}/ui

# --- NATS
# The dispatcher/api reach NATS on the host's published port.
NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222
# The URL injected into agent containers — must be reachable from inside them.
# Single-node (all containers on this machine): host.docker.internal works.
# With a REMOTE worker node it does not (it resolves to the worker itself) —
# use this machine's tailnet IP instead, e.g. nats://100.x.y.z:4222.
NATS_URL_CONTAINER=nats://host.docker.internal:4222

# --- Git front (SSH). Clone URL injected into agent containers; never localhost.
# Same remote-worker rule as NATS_URL_CONTAINER: tailnet IP when a worker exists.
REPO_URL_BASE=ssh://git@host.docker.internal:2222
# Pre-receive hook path as seen INSIDE the ssh container.
HOOK_BIN=/usr/local/bin/chuggernaut

# --- Agent provider defaults (dispatcher refuses to start without PROVIDER).
AGENT_PROVIDER_DEFAULT=claude
AGENT_MODEL_DEFAULT=claude-sonnet-5

# --- Docker fleet.
# Linux with /var/run/docker.sock: leave DOCKER_NODES unset and just set DOCKER_SLOTS.
# macOS + colima: there is NO /var/run/docker.sock, so you MUST set DOCKER_NODES to
# the colima socket (find it via `colima status`), or the dispatcher dies with
# "Socket not found: /var/run/docker.sock". QUOTE the value — the `|` separators are
# shell pipes when this file is sourced. Extra nodes are comma-separated.
#   DOCKER_NODES="local|unix:///Users/<you>/.colima/default/docker.sock|4"
# A `worker` endpoint is a NATS-proxied `chuggernaut worker` daemon on the
# node (spec §3.1) — nothing listens on any network; the daemon dials in.
# Worker-only fleet (README "Worker nodes"): keep the local node registered at
# 0 slots so placement never picks it but failback is a one-line edit:
#   DOCKER_NODES="local|unix:///…/docker.sock|0, nuc|worker|0"
# The slot field means DIFFERENT things per endpoint type (spec §3.1):
#   unix:// or tcp://  — DOCKER_NODES owns that node's capacity, full stop.
#   worker             — membership seed + a PRE-OBSERVATION FALLBACK only. It
#                        applies until the node first reports its own capacity
#                        and can never override one afterwards, so `|worker|0`
#                        is the honest value: the node supplies the real number
#                        on its first ping. This is NOT where you change a
#                        worker's capacity — see docs/runbooks/worker-capacity.md.
# Zeroing an EXISTING deployment's worker seeds is sequenced (that runbook §6):
# the deployed dispatcher must ingest capacity from ping and every daemon must
# be on a build that reports it, or the fleet boots with no capacity at all.
DOCKER_SLOTS=4

# --- Remote worker node (optional; README "Worker nodes").
# SSH destination of the node; update.sh/build-worker.sh build the worker
# daemon + agent images there (context streamed over ssh) and (re)start the
# daemon. Leave unset on single-node deploys.
#WORKER_SSH=worksalot@gumbo-nuc-0
# NATS URL the worker (and its job containers' MCP) reach THIS machine on —
# the tailnet IP form of NATS_URL_CONTAINER.
#WORKER_NATS_URL=nats://100.x.y.z:4222
# Node name for the daemon; must match its DOCKER_NODES entry. Default: nuc.
#CHUG_WORKER_NODE=nuc
# The node's FIRST-BOOT capacity (spec §3.1) — the value it starts at before any
# operator intent exists, not the way capacity is changed. Changing a node's
# concurrency is a runtime command from the Cluster page (or PUT
# /api/v1/platform/fleet/{node}/capacity): no ssh, no rebuild, no restart. See
# docs/runbooks/worker-capacity.md.
# build-worker.sh passes this to the daemon's `docker run` and self-refresh
# carries it forward, so a node whose dispatcher is down still boots at a sane
# number; after a swap the node reports this boot value until the dispatcher
# reconciles the recorded intent back onto it (one scan tick). Unset ⇒ the
# daemon's default of 4. Prod runs air (6cpu/12GiB colima) and nuc (12c/31GB)
# at 2 each.
#WORKER_SLOTS=2
# The CEILING a capacity command is validated against (`WORKER_SLOTS_MAX`, spec
# §3.1) is a knob on the worker DAEMON's env, and it is the one worker knob with
# NO passthrough: neither build-worker.sh nor worker-refresh.sh forwards it, so
# setting it here does nothing. To lower it, add `-e WORKER_SLOTS_MAX=<n>` to the
# daemon's `docker run` by hand — and re-add it after each deploy, since the
# self-refresh swap drops it. Unset ⇒ the node's own CPU count, which is the
# right default unless the CPU count overstates what the node can serve (air:
# 6 CPUs, but 2 concurrent Rust builds is what it sustains). Must be at least 1.
# See docs/runbooks/worker-capacity.md §5.
# Node-local build cache (spec §3.1 "Node-local build caching"): a HOST path on
# the worker. Set ⇒ the daemon turns on sccache and bind-mounts this dir into
# every job container it launches (at /cache/sccache), so cargo builds reuse
# compilation across jobs instead of compiling cold every task. Unset ⇒ caching
# off, nothing changes. build-worker.sh passes it to the daemon's `docker run`
# (env only — no daemon mount; the daemon binds it into sibling containers via
# the docker socket) and self-refresh carries it forward on every deploy.
#
# The HOST path must exist for a job container to launch at all: since #379 the
# cache is a typed mount, so the engine REFUSES a missing source rather than
# creating it empty. build-worker.sh provisions it on the node before it starts
# the daemon (`mkdir -p`, then `sudo -n mkdir -p`), which covers every node built
# or rebuilt through a deploy or through chug-install.sh's worker-join; a daemon
# you start BY HAND needs the mkdir by hand (docs/runbooks/adhoc-deploy.md §1c).
#WORKER_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/chuggernaut/sccache
#
# KVM passthrough, for Android emulator work (design #367; the procedure is
# docs/runbooks/worker-kvm.md). THREE settings — and a DEVICE that is not
# optional. `chug-worker` is itself a container, so the daemon checks for the
# device in ITS OWN view and REFUSES TO START when WORKER_KVM names one it cannot
# see. build-worker.sh therefore adds `--device` to the daemon's own `docker run`
# whenever WORKER_KVM is on, and worker-refresh.sh's swap carries that device
# forward by inspecting the live container. Setting the env WITHOUT the device is
# a node-down, not a degraded node: the daemon refuses, `--restart=always` loops
# the refusal, and the node leaves the fleet — which is why these vars and the
# device are threaded through the same two scripts and never set by hand.
# Unset ⇒ no passthrough at all; nothing about a non-Android node changes.
#
# 1/true/on passes the node's /dev/kvm; an ABSOLUTE PATH names another device
# node; 0/false/off is off (the setting rides, no device is attached, and a
# self-refresh of such a node swaps normally). Surrounding whitespace is trimmed,
# the daemon's own reading. Anything else build-worker.sh refuses, before it
# replaces a working daemon with one that could not boot.
#WORKER_KVM=1
# WHO may use it: `owner/project` entries, comma-separated (the JOB_PROJECT
# shape). FAIL-CLOSED — unset or empty grants NOBODY, so enabling KVM on a node
# and granting it to a project stay two separate acts. A malformed or repeated
# entry is a hard daemon config error, never a grant that silently never matches.
#WORKER_KVM_PROJECTS=acme/beacon
# The node's Android SDK, bind-mounted read-only into an allow-listed launch
# beside the device. Name the operator's ACTIVATION-MAINTAINED STABLE path (the
# `environment.etc` entry in the node's configuration.nix), NEVER a /nix/store
# path: a store path is content-hashed, so a pinned one keeps testing the
# PREVIOUS SDK after a bump and then fails with an unattributable ENOENT once the
# store is collected. The daemon refuses a store hash outright (design #367
# §3.5). Unset ⇒ /var/lib/chuggernaut/android-sdk.
#WORKER_ANDROID_SDK_DIR=/etc/chug/android-sdk
#
# Per-task nix GC roots (spec §3.1 "Node-local nix GC roots", design #373 P1;
# the procedure is docs/runbooks/worker-kvm.md §7). The mounts above hand a task
# store paths that NOTHING roots, so the node's weekly nix-gc can collect a path
# out from under a running task. Set this and the daemon realises the node's
# declared toolchain before an allow-listed launch and holds an indirect GC root
# over it, named by task id, for exactly that task's lifetime — released when the
# container is removed, and reaped later if a worker died holding it.
#
# It names a WORKER-WRITABLE host directory. build-worker.sh provisions it
# (`mkdir -p`, then `sudo -n mkdir -p`) and mounts four paths into chug-worker
# itself: /nix/store read-only, the profiles tree read-only, the daemon socket
# dir READ-WRITE (connecting to a unix socket needs write on the inode), and this
# dir read-write at the SAME path — the nix daemon resolves a root path in its
# own namespace. On a node that attaches a KVM device it mounts a FIFTH: the
# DIRECTORY HOLDING WORKER_ANDROID_SDK_DIR, read-only at the same path. Never
# that path itself — `nix-store --realise` resolves its argument CLIENT-side,
# inside chug-worker, and a bind whose source IS the operator's symlink has it
# resolved away by mount(2), leaving the store path's content at a non-store
# path the client refuses. Hence the shape requirement: the stable path must be
# a DIRECT absolute symlink into the store (a systemd.tmpfiles `L+` line), not a
# NixOS environment.etc entry, which hops through /etc/static. build-worker.sh
# refuses a deploy whose path is any other shape, and the daemon re-derives the
# same property from inside the container at boot.
# The daemon refuses to start when any of them is missing from its
# own view, so a deploy that cannot provision the dir REFUSES rather than leaving
# a node looping on --restart=always. Unset ⇒ no realise, no roots, no mounts.
#WORKER_NIX_GCROOTS_DIR=/var/lib/chuggernaut/gcroots
# The client, and why it is a PROFILES path: chug-worker is long-lived and docker
# resolves a bind source host-side at create, so a client resolved into the store
# is pinned to the generation current at the last swap — which nix.gc's
# --delete-older-than collects, out from under the running daemon. The profiles
# tree is itself a GC root. A store-hash value is refused outright.
# Unset ⇒ /nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix-store.
#WORKER_NIX_CLIENT=/nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix-store
# The node's nix daemon socket (0666 on a stock node, so no uid mapping is
# needed). Unset ⇒ /nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/socket.
#WORKER_NIX_DAEMON_SOCKET=/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/socket
# The store PREFIX — what build-worker.sh mounts read-only, and what the daemon's
# boot check requires the toolchain path to resolve into. Only a node that
# relocated its store (nix's own NIX_STORE_DIR) sets this.
# Unset ⇒ /nix/store.
#WORKER_NIX_STORE_DIR=/nix/store
# The realise BOUND, in seconds. The realise runs before the task exists, so no
# resources.task_timeout covers it and an unbounded one would hang the launch
# path. Breaking it FAILS THE LAUNCH loudly (never a retryable no-capacity: a
# realise that broke the bound is not faster on a retry).
#
# 1..45 ONLY, and both the daemon and build-worker.sh refuse anything else. The
# realise runs INSIDE the `launch` RPC, which the dispatcher abandons after 60s
# (store::worker::OP_TIMEOUT) — a longer bound is never reached: the task fails
# on worker transport, the loud named failure above is never seen, and this node
# goes on to launch a container nobody is waiting for. A toolchain that is
# legitimately slower to substitute than this wants a warming job (design #373
# Decision 5), not a bigger number here. Unset ⇒ 30.
#WORKER_NIX_REALISE_TIMEOUT_SECS=30
#
# NO-SSH self-refresh (spec §3.1) — the path prod uses, since the Mini cannot
# ssh a tagged worker. On every deploy, update.sh requests each `worker`-endpoint
# node in DOCKER_NODES to rebuild its images at the deployed SHA and swap its
# daemon (job containers survive). The daemon fetches the build context ITSELF
# over the ssh front, so it needs two vars on ITS env: WORKER_REFRESH_GIT_URL
# (ssh://git@<tailnet-ip>:2222/<owner>/<platform-repo>.git) and WORKER_GIT_KEY
# (its ssh key). Set them HERE (below) — build-worker.sh passes both into the
# daemon's `docker run` and the self-refresh swap carries them forward — or, on
# a node created some other way, add them to that `docker run` by hand. A node that
# fails to refresh is a WARNING with its version surfaced, never a deploy failure.
# A node with NEITHER set can't refresh — the deploy now says so LOUDLY
# ("node <name>: refresh SKIPPED — no git credential") instead of no-oping quietly.
#
# ENROLLMENT — mint the node's read-only git credential on the Mini:
#   chuggernaut admin worker-git-key --node <name> --project <owner>/<platform-repo>
# writes worker_git + worker_git-cert.pub (a long-lived read-only cert scoped to
# just that repo). Install both under the node's ~/chuggernaut-worker/keys/, then
# set the two keys below (WORKER_GIT_KEY already defaults to the path the daemon
# sees them at) and re-run build-worker.sh to hand them to the daemon.
#WORKER_REFRESH_GIT_URL=ssh://git@100.x.y.z:2222/acme/chuggernaut.git
#WORKER_GIT_KEY=/data/keys/worker_git

# --- Platform self-repo (deploy-drift reporting).
# `owner/project` of the platform's OWN source repo — the chuggernaut project
# this dispatcher is deployed from (as it is named on this platform, not on the
# GitHub mirror). Each scan tick the dispatcher resolves that project's main tip
# and how many commits the deployed SHA is behind it, which is what the Cluster
# page's drift banner shows ("Prod is N commits behind main").
# UNSET ⇒ the banner reads "Deploy drift unavailable — main tip unknown"; nothing
# else changes and nothing fails. chug-install.sh writes this line for you when
# you import the platform repo (`project-import`), so set it by hand only on a
# deployment whose platform repo was imported some other way.
#SELF_REPO=acme/chuggernaut

# --- API / UI
# The api runs natively under launchd (com.chuggernaut.api / run-api.sh), the
# same `chuggernaut` binary the host builds every deploy; it binds 127.0.0.1:8080
# (Tailscale Serve / cloudflared front it — see README §5). run-api.sh reads
# NATS_URL/KEYS_DIR/UI_ROOT/SESSION_TTL from this file; only SESSION_TTL is
# api-specific.
SESSION_TTL=24h

# --- Container image tag built by deploy/prod/build.sh (chuggernaut/ssh:$TAG etc).
CHUG_IMAGE_TAG=prod
# Docker compose network name (project 'chuggernaut' => chuggernaut_default),
# used by backup-r2.sh to run nats-box on the same network.
NATS_NETWORK=chuggernaut_default

# --- Backups → Cloudflare R2
# age recipient (PUBLIC key) the backup tarball is encrypted to. The matching
# private key is kept OFFLINE (password manager), never on this machine.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# rclone remote:bucket the encrypted tarball is pushed to.
RCLONE_REMOTE=r2:chuggernaut-backups
# How many encrypted tarballs to keep on local disk before pruning.
BACKUP_LOCAL_KEEP=24
